diagnosis_text:
Affected component: the L2/L1 link for router4’s LAN gateway interface (192.168.200.1) toward the access switch. Mechanism: brief physical carrier drops on the router4 eth0 <-> switch4 eth0 link cause the default gateway to disappear for a few seconds, interrupting user traffic; once the link returns, connectivity resumes, matching the “random dropouts for a few seconds” symptom. Localization: router4 eth0 (192.168.200.1) connected to switch4 eth0 (sw4_r4). Root cause: the physical link between router4 eth0 and switch4 eth0 is flapping (not congestion/overload). Direct evidence: on router4, /sys/class/net/eth0/carrier_changes = 2 with a recent carrier mtime (2026-07-30 04:00:29 UTC), and on switch4 eth0 the same carrier_changes = 2 with the same recent mtime, proving a recent up/down/up event on that exact link. Current pings from PC11–PC13 to 192.168.200.1 show 0% loss, and router4 eth0 shows no errors/drops and zero queue backlog (tc fq_codel), ruling out overload. Corrective fix: stabilize the physical segment between router4 eth0 and switch4 eth0—inspect/replace patch leads, reseat interfaces, or move to known-good ports. If this is a virtual lab cabling issue, re-create the link. Optionally hard-set speed/duplex consistently and monitor carrier_changes to confirm it remains stable (should not increment further). === END ===